## What ships

A release is a single executable, produced by `cargo build --release -p zet` for
`x86_64-pc-windows-msvc`. Rust links its dependencies statically, so the binary contains
**92 third-party crates** from crates.io and **two embedded font files**. There is no
sidecar library or data file to distribute next to it, which is why this notice is a document
rather than a directory in the archive.

The inventory below was generated from `Cargo.lock` by resolving `zet`'s dependency edges for the
Windows target — `cargo tree -p zet --edges normal --target x86_64-pc-windows-msvc` — against zet
0.1.0. Two categories of crate that a reading of the lockfile alone would count are left out of
it, and [What does not ship](#what-does-not-ship) says which and why. Versions are the locked ones
rather than the newest available. Where a crate offers a choice of licences (`OR`), any of the
offered terms may be relied on, and every text involved is reproduced at the end of this file;
where a component requires both (`AND`), both apply.

## What does not ship

**Build-script dependencies.** Build scripts are compiled for the machine doing the building and
run while `zet.exe` is assembled; their own code is not part of the result. The Windows build
runs build scripts belonging to `glutin_wgl_sys`, `naga`, `num-traits`, `wgpu`, `wgpu-core`,
`wgpu-hal`, and `winit`, and those scripts in turn use:

| Crate | Version | Licence | Needed by |
